@* A two-column list of properties: what each one is called, and what it is set to.

   The grid lays the rows out and shows what is wrong with them; the input of a row is whatever the
   feature puts there. Handing the grid a description of the fields instead would make it draw the
   inputs too, and the first field with its own labels or its own visibility rule would need a way
   around it — so the layout is shared and the controls are not. *@

<div class="property-grid">
    @* The rows need the columns of this grid to fall into, so the grid is cascaded for a row to
       find — and to miss when it is placed anywhere else. *@
    <CascadingValue Value="this" IsFixed="true">
        @this.ChildContent
    </CascadingValue>
</div>

@code {
    /// <summary>
    /// Gets or sets the <see cref="PropertyGridRow"/> content shown by the grid.
    /// </summary>
    [Parameter]
    [EditorRequired]
    public RenderFragment? ChildContent { get; set; }
}
